Verification
Test Endpoints Using curl
Open a new terminal (keep the Flask server running in the first terminal):

# Test health check
curl http://localhost:5000/api/health

# Test GET all books
curl http://localhost:5000/api/books

# Test GET single book
curl http://localhost:5000/api/books/1

# Test POST - Create new book
curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/api/books \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "title": "The Pragmatic Programmer",
    "author": "Hunt and Thomas",
    "year": 1999,
    "isbn": "978-0201616224"
  }'

# Test PUT - Update book
curl -X PUT http://localhost:5000/api/books/1 \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "title": "Updated Title",
    "author": "Updated Author",
    "year": 2023,
    "isbn": "978-1234567890"
  }'

# Test DELETE
curl -X DELETE http://localhost:5000/api/books/1

# Verify deletion
curl http://localhost:5000/api/books/1
Expected Results
GET /api/books: Returns JSON array with status 200
GET /api/books/1: Returns single book object or 404
POST /api/books: Returns new book ID with status 201
PUT /api/books/1: Returns success message with status 200
DELETE /api/books/1: Returns success message with status 200
Subsequent GET after DELETE should return 404
Verify Database Changes
# Check database directly
sqlite3 library.db "SELECT * FROM books;"
Troubleshooting Tips
Port Already in Use

# Find process using port 5000
sudo lsof -i :5000
# Kill the process
kill -9 <PID>
Module Not Found Error

# Ensure virtual environment is activated
source ~/rest-api-lab/venv/bin/activate
# Reinstall dependencies
pip install flask flask-cors
Database Locked Error

Close all SQLite connections
Restart the Flask application
JSON Decode Error

Verify Content-Type header is set to application/json
Check JSON syntax in curl commands
404 on All Endpoints

Verify Flask app is running
Check route definitions match URL patterns
Ensure no typos in endpoint paths
Conclusion
You have successfully:

Built a RESTful API with Flask framework
Implemented CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete)
Connected REST endpoints to a SQLite database
Handled HTTP requests and responses with appropriate status codes
Tested API functionality using curl commands
This lab demonstrates core concepts used in modern web services and microservices architectures. RESTful APIs are fundamental to building scalable, maintainable applications that separate frontend and backend concerns. The patterns you've learned here apply to production systems using more robust databases like PostgreSQL or MySQL.

